<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdullahi dan Fodio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1766–<a href="/years/1829">1829</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdullahi dan Fodio was the younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> and a leading scholar, poet and commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> that created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> in the early nineteenth century. After the conquests, he was given authority over the western part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwandu-emirate">Gwandu</a>. His writings are an important source for the movement's ideals and early history.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abdullahi was born in the small village of Maganimi. His father, Muhammad bin Uthman, was a local teacher, scribe and religious leader whose learning in Islamic law earned him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> nickname Fodiye, and his mother, Hauwa bint Muhammad, taught him the basics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. By 13 he had reportedly memorised the Qur'an. His brother Usman then taught him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> law and Arabic poetry, and from his early teens Abdullahi went with Usman on preaching tours across Hausaland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunfa">Yunfa</a>, the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a>, ordered Usman and his family to leave, many followers made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">hijra</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> began. Abdullahi, as Usman's waziri, commanded the jihadist army in the campaigns of 1804 and, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuaregs</a> of Adar and Aïr, led the siege of the Gobir capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alkalawa">Alkalawa</a> in November 1804. In 1805 he and Ali Jedo jointly led the campaign into the Kingdom of Kebbi, whose fertile lands could feed the community.</p><p>On the night of 4 October 1807 he left the army with five companions to travel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, believing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> had turned from religious revival to material ends. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, where rival Fulbe clans contended for power, he wrote Diya' al-hukkam, later regarded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a> as its second constitution. He rejoined the jihad late in 1807. Around December 1809 Usman left him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwandu-emirate">Gwandu</a> to carry on the war in the west, where he occupied Dandi, Kamba and Zaberma and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Abdullahi left about eighty writings and was the only author of the 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> to write exegesis of the Qur'an (Tafsir). His two-volume Diya' al-ta'wil, 747 folios long, was read across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>; in it he argued that rulers must govern through al-Shura, consultation, and may be removed if they do not. Tazyin al-waraqat, a collection of his poems on the campaigns completed in 1813, is a historical source for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>.</p><p>In Diya' al-sultan (1812) he warned of the dangers of succession from father to son, probably because he thought Usman favoured Bello; Usman replied in Najm al-ikhwan in 1813. Initiated by his teacher Jibril ibn Umar into the Shadhiliyah, Khalwatiya and Qadiriyah orders, he followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a> of Abu Hamid al-Ghazali. After his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nana-asmau">Nana Asma'u</a> wrote an elegy for him, and many of his Fulfulde poems are still sung in Adamawa and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abdullahi dan Fodio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Muhammad Bello and brother <b>Abdullahi dan Fodio</b> led the campaigns against Gobir, while flagbearers such as Sulaimanu in Kano, Musa in Zaria and Modibbo Adama in Adamawa carved out new emirates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the victory of the jihad, Usman dan Fodio divided responsibility for his realm around 1812: his son Muhammad Bello took the east from Sokoto, while his brother <b>Abdullahi dan Fodio</b> took the west from Gwandu, a town he had fortified in 1808.</blockquote><a href="/state/gwandu-emirate">Gwandu Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
